def point_in_triangle(p, a, b, c):
    def cross(o, a, b):
        return (a[0] - o[0]) * (b[1] - o[1]) - (a[1] - o[1]) * (b[0] - o[0])
    d1 = cross(a, b, p)
    d2 = cross(b, c, p)
    d3 = cross(c, a, p)
    has_neg = (d1 < 0) or (d2 < 0) or (d3 < 0)
    has_pos = (d1 > 0) or (d2 > 0) or (d3 > 0)
    return not (has_neg and has_pos)
```

The sign of the vector cross product determines on which side of each edge of the triangle the point lies. The three edges being {{\$a, b\$}}, {{\$b, c\$}}, and {{\$c, a\$}}, a point lies inside the triangle or on its boundary exactly when it cannot be on opposite sides of all three edges at once, that is, when $\${d_1}\$ \$, \$d_2}$, and \$d_3}$ are not all positive and not all negative together. Points on the boundary produce exactly one zero cross product and the loop still runs: \$has_neg\$ and \$has_pos\$ then become true only when two of the products have negative and positive signs, and the condition returns true.

The third assertion, where a triangle has a repeated vertex at the origin, would collapse into the segment from the origin to (4, 0) to (0, 4), and the origin lies on its midpoint, which is inside the collapsed shape. The second assertion places a point well outside a right triangle at those three vertices, and the function returns False. The first assertion asks about the point (1, 1): all three signed areas are positive, the loop returns false, and the function returns True.

