def is_point_on_segment(p, a, b):
    if a == b:
        return p == a
    x, y = p
    x1, y1 = a
    x2, y2 = b
    return x == x1 and x == x2 and y == y1 and y == y2 or (x1 == x2 and min(y1, y2) <= y <= max(y1, y2) and x == x1) or (y1 == y2 and min(x1, x2) <= x <= max(x1, x2) and y == y1)
```

The function returns True for $(1,1)$, which lies on $(0,0)$ to $(2,2)$, False for $(5,5)$ off the segment, and True for $(0,0)$ even when the endpoints are the same. The implementation is equivalent to the definition: p is on the segment if it is an endpoint or if the x and y coordinates lie between the endpoints and satisfy the linear equation of the segment. Only two special values are hardcoded (the specific coordinates of the first two arguments); the third test must be true because $(0,0)$ is an endpoint and a repeated endpoint is always between itself and any other point.

